.TH FL-OSK 1 "November 2026" "fldesktop 0.1.0" "fldesktop"
.SH NAME
fl-osk \- on-screen keyboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfl-osk\fR [\fB\-\-layout\fR \fIus\fR|\fIde\fR|\fIfr\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-toggle\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
An on-screen keyboard for touch screens and computers without a keyboard.
It types into the focused window through the Wayland virtual-keyboard
protocol and never takes the focus itself. \fBfl-wm\fR(1) docks it above the
panel and keeps windows out of its area.
.PP
Shift applies to the next key; tap it twice for Caps Lock. The \fB?123\fR key
switches to numbers and symbols. Starting \fBfl-osk\fR while it runs closes
the running keyboard, so the same command (or the panel's keyboard button)
shows and hides it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-layout\fR \fIname\fR
Key layout: \fBus\fR (QWERTY), \fBde\fR (QWERTZ) or \fBfr\fR (AZERTY).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-toggle\fR
Do not close an instance that is already running.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/osk.ini\fR
\fB[Keyboard]\fR \fBLayout\fR, \fBHeight\fR (pixels) and \fBMaxWidth\fR.
.TP
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/panel.ini\fR
\fB[Panel] KeyboardButton=auto|always|never\fR; \fBauto\fR shows the panel
button when no physical keyboard is present.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfl-greet\fR(1), \fBfl-panel\fR(1), \fBfl-wm\fR(1)
